    WEEK 01 / ESSAY ARCHIVE
  </p>

  <h3>NO IFS, BUTS, HOWEVER YOU CAN HAVE ANDS</h3>

  <p>
    It was a cold day in December, and I was sitting on an anticipated Zoom call with a psychiatrist. “Describe your symptoms.” I described my symptoms. “You have bipolar I disorder.” <em>Okay.</em> I fought this diagnosis for years, was ashamed of it, hated myself over it, but eventually came to accept it. <em>I have bipolar disorder.</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    It was November 22, 2023. I was standing at a podium in a courtroom in the Boulder County courthouse. I was listening to the words the judge was saying, but they weren’t registering. “What’s going on?” I asked my lawyer. A bailiff was approaching me. “You’re being arrested.” He had handcuffs. “At least let me say goodbye,” I said. So, I said goodbye to my parents, and I was hauled off to jail. In that moment, I knew that there was nothing I could do about my circumstances. I accepted the fact that I was going to jail. <em>I am incarcerated.</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    In both instances—eventually or right away—I practiced radical acceptance before knowing what radical acceptance was. Radical acceptance is fully acknowledging reality as it exists, without fighting the fact that it exists, but accepting the circumstances doesn’t mean liking them, approving of them, or resigning yourself to an unchangeable future. Once you’ve acknowledged reality, you can focus on what remains within your control. My bipolar diagnosis took me years to accept, yet I was able to accept my incarceration right away—before the handcuffs were on. I referred to the diagnosis as a “life sentence” for a long time, while the jail sentence was only for six months. There was an ending to that. Bipolar disorder has no end for me. The prospect of spending my life managing something I hadn't asked for or done anything to warrant sat wrong with me for a long time, especially something stigmatized that required me to reconsider my understanding of myself. Compared to my diagnosis, my arrest was an immediate reality I couldn’t escape, yet it had an end date.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now, I actively manage my bipolar disorder with things such as therapy, medication, regular psychiatry visits, adequate sleep, and mood tracking. When I was incarcerated, I worked to make meaningful use of my time by exploring my spirituality with the jail chaplain, reading self-help books, taking classes, and journaling. These truths aren’t mutually exclusive: acceptance doesn’t mean abandoning the possibility of change. These are examples of dialectical thinking. Dialectics involve recognizing that two seemingly opposing things can be true at the same time. I am in recovery and accept myself, but I still want to improve. I acknowledge my wrongdoings, but I recognize my capacity to change. I value my independence, but I accept help from others. I experience difficult emotions, but I find moments of peace. The important piece is the word <em>and</em> instead of the word <em>but</em>. There is room for both statements in reality. With bipolar disorder, acceptance doesn’t mean I decided to stop managing my condition. With incarceration, acceptance didn’t mean sitting around waiting for my release date. I read, wrote, studied, attended therapy, and developed my spiritual beliefs. I accepted my circumstances without accepting that I had to remain passive within them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Bringing this idea to recovery, I needed to surrender and accept that I cannot reliably control my drinking to give myself greater control over my life. Recognizing that I am an alcoholic doesn’t mean I’m powerless over every aspect of my life. In treatment, I learned that trying to control my alcohol consumption wasn’t a realistic or sustainable option for me. I accepted that I can’t reliably control my drinking <em>and</em> I can take responsibility for choosing sobriety. I believed I could control my drinking after my first DUI, but I failed to recognize that controlling my alcohol consumption wasn’t working because I was under the delusion that my drinking was affecting “just me.” Accepting my addiction felt different from accepting my bipolar diagnosis for this reason. I found sobriety gave me more freedom than trying to control my drinking since I don’t have to worry about all of the alcoholic planning, hangovers, and worries that come along with drinking. I recognize my limitations now and practice a healthy, alcohol-free lifestyle in which drinking does not even cross my mind. That is true freedom. I am still an alcoholic even if I accept it, though. It still requires management—it requires good choices.
  </p>

  <p>
    As defined, radical acceptance isn’t approval, even when reality is painful, unfair, or the result of your own actions or decisions. I accept that I have three DUIs, but I don’t believe my actions are acceptable at all. I am responsible, and I am accountable for my actions and need to pay for what I have done. I acknowledge I have an addiction without defining my entire identity by it. I have bipolar disorder, but that is not my entire personality—I don’t like to say <em>I am bipolar</em>, but I catch myself sometimes. Acceptance describes my relationship with reality, not my moral judgment of what happened.
  </p>

  <p>
    Accountability starts within. It goes along with guilt, or it can begin with shame. Guilt says, “I did something wrong.” Shame says, “There is something fundamentally wrong with me.” Accountability steps in and says, “I recognize what I did, acknowledge its consequences, and take responsibility for my actions.” These aren’t mutually exclusive emotions or responses; distinguishing them explains why acceptance doesn’t mean self-condemnation. Someone can regret their behavior without deciding they’re incapable of becoming a better person.
  </p>

  <p>
    When I was arrested in that courtroom, I accepted my circumstances immediately. Processing the decisions that brought me there took a bit longer. Thinking about what I did helped me focus on what I could do differently. Journaling and therapy also helped me understand accountability, as did being in that courtroom. I learned that I was not accountable that day—I needed to step up better. I lived with a lot of guilt while within those concrete walls, dwelling in my own thoughts, eventually accepting the consequences that had put me on a bunk with a mat that barely functioned as a bed. Accepting the past and my actions allowed me to focus on the present and plan for the future. I didn’t reach complete forgiveness or resolve every emotion while I was there, but I started the journey and took some steps. I regretted my decisions and felt a lot of remorse for all of the people I hurt and put in difficult positions, and I accepted that I made them. I didn’t have to choose between condemning my behavior and accepting my past. Both can be true. I could not change what I did, but I could change what I did next.
  </p>

  <p>
    Walking a path in the middle involves recognizing two seemingly opposing perspectives that can both contain truth. It doesn’t mean compromising or choosing a position exactly halfway between two extremes. Sometimes it means understanding that both needs are legitimate and finding a way to honor them. For example, I can be independent and still need help from other people. Neither cancels the other out. I can appreciate who I am while changing behaviors that aren’t serving me. I can experience an intense emotion without assuming my interpretation of a situation is accurate. The middle path isn’t always comfortable—sometimes two legitimate needs really do compete.
  </p>

  <p>
    I was sitting in a small classroom with a group of women and an instructor. I forget what we were even talking about, but women were mentioning different things they did in their addiction. I identified with all of them: hiding alcohol, planning, hangovers, driving drunk. Literally <em>all</em> of them. Silently, I realized: <em>I am truly an addict.</em> I had known, but it hit me full force while at rehab. I belonged there, and I was getting the help that I finally needed. <em>I am a bipolar addict who went to jail.</em> This is not who I am; these are just parts of what I deal with or what happened to me. And I accept these things. Some require management, others need to be dealt with, and others belong in my memories. I know one thing that is true due to treatment, loved ones, my spirituality, and radical acceptance: <em>I am free.</em>
  </p>

